# Getting started with Upwell 0.20

Upwell `0.20.0` is a published pre-1.0 release. Use the `upwell` package and `upwell` imports shown here; this release continues under the Upwell name rather than an older package spelling.

## Add the facade crate

Create a Rust binary and depend on the released facade crate:

<Example title="Start an Upwell 0.20 application">

```toml
[dependencies]
upwell = "0.20.0"
```

Declare a component that the application can construct:

```rust plain
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[component]
struct Greeting;

impl Greeting {
    fn message(&self) -> &'static str {
        "Hello from Upwell 0.20"
    }
}
```

</Example>

## Declare the host

In 0.20, `app!` takes the application builder form. The builder is where a binary supplies its name and registrations; the macro supplies the generated application host.

<Example title="Use the 0.20 builder-form app macro">

```rust plain
use upwell::prelude::*;

app! {
    App::builder("hello")
        .with_component(Greeting)
}
```

</Example>

Run the generated binary with Cargo. The exact generated command-line surface is part of the 0.20 host, so consult `--help` in the application being built rather than carrying assumptions from later releases.

## Read the 1.0 guides separately

The current guides describe the 1.0 application model and should not be used as syntax references for a 0.20 program. When planning an upgrade, start with [migrating to 1.0](/docs/0.20.0/migration-to-1-0) and then use the [1.0 getting-started guide](/docs/1.0.0/getting-started).
